 "answer": {
  "cell_layout": "Cell allocation is 0x30 bytes (dealloc size in the Arc drop glue 0x5bc2a80): strong count at +0x00, weak count at +0x08, payload S at +0x10: {context A at +0x10 (its own Arc count at A+0x00 and a second atomic counter at A+0x18, incremented by the spawn body at 0x15b835a and decremented by the payload drop 0x5bc3bf0, which also sets a flag byte at A+0x20 when it discards a live pair), state at +0x18, data at +0x20, vtable at +0x28}. The spawn body initialises state to 0 and leaves the pair slot uninitialised; the pair is only ever read when state is nonzero (dispatcher drain, Arc drop glue, payload drop) or taken by the join body after the thread has finished.",
  "payload_shapes": "Normal path: r14 is zeroed at dispatcher entry (0x15b9083), so the stored pair is {data = NULL, vtable = r15} -- an empty trait object under a live vtable, with state 1 as the completion marker the join body tests (bit 0) before returning the pair in rax:rdx. Catch path: after the begin-catch body (slot 0x64df4c0 -> 0x1680cab), the helper behind slot 0x64dfe88 (0x5bb19f0) checks the panic object's \"MOZ\\0RUST\" magic and returns the enclosed Box fat pointer (data from object +0x28, vtable from object +0x30) in rax:rdx; the dispatcher moves it to r14:r15 (0x15b9170 / 0x15b918e), drains any pair already in the cell, and the same finish block stores the panic payload pair.",
  "producers": "The only producer of this cell instance is the dispatcher itself. Its finish block (state := 1, data := r14, vtable := r15, then a strong-count decrement on the cell) runs on every exit: the fall-through copy at 0x15b90da and the landing-pad copy at 0x15b9141. The completion-store idiom occurs in exactly 13 bodies in the whole .text (idiom census); none of the other 12 is a producer of this instance: four are the sibling dispatchers of the same table family, five are sibling dispatchers of other closures whose cells share this cell's Arc drop glue (slot 0x64dfe60), two are dispatchers of two different cell types (their own drop-glue slots), and two write the idiom onto their own stack frames only. The cell's other accesses are not productions: the spawn body initialises it, the join body takes the pair out (state := 0), and the Arc/payload drop paths drain it if a pair is still present.",
  "r15_origin": "Pinned from the producer side. The dispatcher contains exactly one r15-writing instruction, the catch-path mov %rdx,%r15 at 0x15b918e, so on the normal path the stored r15 is the caller's value, preserved across both adapter calls. The only caller is the thread start routine 0x5ca44a0 (no direct call sites exist; the thread-create body 0x5c87b50 passes it to pthread_create as the start routine, lea at 0x5c87c19). The start routine loads r15 from package +0x10 at 0x5ca45a6 and does not write r15 before the call *0x18(%r15) at 0x5ca45d7. Package +0x10 is written by the spawn body 0x15b80e0: the package is a fresh 0x18-byte allocation {thread Arc at +0x00, closure data at +0x08, vtable base at +0x10}, and the vtable base stored at 0x15b8461.. is the lea 0x6400530 result of 0x15b8436 -- this dispatcher's own static-table entry. So on the normal path packet +0x28 receives the entry vtable base 0x6400530, produced by the spawn body; the lea census shows each of the 11 entries is loaded by exactly one lea, in its own spawn body."
 },
